<!-- ==================== COVER ==================== -->
<div class="cover">
  <div class="label">Trend Analysis Report</div>
  <h1>AI Impact on the Global Job Market: Risk, Salary &amp; Skills Trend Analysis (2015–2035)</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive examination of how AI automation is reshaping the global workforce — from risk exposure and salary dynamics to skill demand and job survival — across 10 roles, 6 countries, and 3 experience levels.</p>
  <div class="cover-meta">
    <span><strong>Data Source:</strong> Kaggle (shree0910/ai-job-risk-and-salary-dataset-20152035)</span>
    <span><strong>Records:</strong> 12,343</span>
    <span><strong>Period:</strong> 2015 – 2035</span>
    <span><strong>Date:</strong> May 2026</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- ==================== EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW ==================== -->
<div class="section" id="executive-overview">
  <h2>Executive Overview</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">A high-level synthesis of the most critical findings for executive leadership and workforce strategists.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value negative">0.508</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg AI Risk Score (2035)</div>
      <div class="kpi-trend down">▲ +72% from 0.295 in 2015</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value positive">$36,763</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Median Global Salary (2035)</div>
      <div class="kpi-trend up">▲ +51.6% from $24,247 in 2015</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value negative">83.7%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Job Survival Rate (2035)</div>
      <div class="kpi-trend down">▼ -7.4pp from 91.1% in 2015</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">80.2</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg Skill Demand Score (2035)</div>
      <div class="kpi-trend neutral">▲ +1.3 from 78.9 in 2015</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">14.8M</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Job Openings (2035)</div>
      <div class="kpi-trend neutral">+5.9% from 14.0M in 2015</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:20px;margin-top:24px;">
    <div class="exec-insight">
      <h4>1. AI Risk Accelerates Sharply After 2030</h4>
      <p>The average AI risk score climbs from 0.295 in 2015 to 0.508 in 2035, with the steepest acceleration occurring after 2030. By 2035, the proportion of high-risk jobs reaches 18.6% of all records — up from negligible levels in the historical period.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="exec-insight">
      <h4>2. A Tale of Two Workforces Emerges</h4>
      <p>AI Researchers, ML Engineers, and Cybersecurity Analysts maintain 100% survival rates throughout the horizon. In stark contrast, Business Analysts (43.0%) and Data Analysts (56.5%) face severe AI disruption risk, with survival rates dropping significantly in projected years.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="exec-insight">
      <h4>3. Salary &times; Risk: A Strong Inverse Relationship</h4>
      <p>High-risk roles command a median salary of $21,683 — 37% less than low-risk roles at $34,105. The correlation between AI risk score and salary is r = -0.24, confirming that automation-vulnerable jobs are systematically lower-paid.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="exec-insight">
      <h4>4. Job Openings Reallocate, Don't Disappear</h4>
      <p>Despite rising AI risk, aggregate job openings hold relatively steady — declining only marginally from 14.0M to 14.8M across the horizon. However, openings shift away from DevOps Engineer (-27.8%) and Data Scientist (-13.3%) toward Product Manager (+45.4%) and Cybersecurity Analyst (+49.1%).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="exec-insight" style="grid-column: 1 / -1;">
      <h4>5. Education Alone Is Not a Shield — Skill Choice Matters More</h4>
      <p>Median salaries are nearly identical across Bachelor's ($31,569), Master's ($31,434), and PhD ($31,672) levels, suggesting salary is driven more by role and skill choice than by degree. However, PhD holders combined with Senior experience achieve the highest survival rate (94.9%).</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ==================== AUTOMATION RISK LANDSCAPE ==================== -->
<div class="section" id="automation-risk-landscape">
  <h2>AI Automation Risk Landscape</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Distribution of AI risk scores and risk categories across job titles, countries, and experience levels. Identifies the roles and regions most and least vulnerable to automation.</p>

  <h3>Global AI Risk Trend (2015–2035)</h3>
  <div class="chart-container" style="height:380px;">
    <canvas id="chartRiskTrend"></canvas>
  </div>
  <p class="small-note">The average AI risk score across all roles climbs 72.1% over the 21-year horizon, from 0.295 to 0.508. The pace intensifies after 2030.</p>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h4>AI Risk Score by Job Title (2015 vs 2035)</h4>
      <div class="chart-container" style="height:340px;">
        <canvas id="chartRiskByJob"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h4>AI Risk Category Distribution</h4>
      <div class="chart-container" style="height:340px;">
        <canvas id="chartRiskCategory"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Risk Profile by Job Title</h3>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Job Title</th><th class="num">Risk 2015</th><th class="num">Risk 2035</th><th class="num">&Delta;</th><th>Risk Category</th><th>Vulnerability</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>AI Researcher</td><td class="num">0.050</td><td class="num">0.250</td><td class="num">+0.200</td><td><span class="badge risk-low">Low Risk</span></td><td>Minimal — AI-specialist roles benefit from demand for AI expertise</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ML Engineer</td><td class="num">0.150</td><td class="num">0.350</td><td class="num">+0.200</td><td><span class="badge risk-low">Low Risk</span></td><td>Low — builds and maintains the automation systems themselves</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cybersecurity Analyst</td><td class="num">0.150</td><td class="num">0.350</td><td class="num">+0.200</td><td><span class="badge risk-low">Low Risk</span></td><td>Low — AI-driven cyber threats increase demand for defenders</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DevOps Engineer</td><td class="num">0.200</td><td class="num">0.400</td><td class="num">+0.200</td><td><span class="badge risk-med">Medium Risk</span></td><td>Moderate — infrastructure automation partially substitutes human effort</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud Engineer</td><td class="num">0.250</td><td class="num">0.450</td><td class="num">+0.200</td><td><span class="badge risk-med">Medium Risk</span></td><td>Moderate — cloud management increasingly automated</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data Scientist</td><td class="num">0.250</td><td class="num">0.450</td><td class="num">+0.200</td><td><span class="badge risk-med">Medium Risk</span></td><td>Moderate — AutoML tools compress some analytics workflows</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Product Manager</td><td class="num">0.300</td><td class="num">0.500</td><td class="num">+0.200</td><td><span class="badge risk-med">Medium Risk</span></td><td>Moderate — strategic and interpersonal skills resist full automation</td></tr>
